+## `problem`
+
+Use `problem()` to respond with [RFC 9457](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457) problem details. It accepts the same fields as `HTTPError` plus `type` and `instance`, and returns an `HTTPError`.
+
+```js
+import { problem } from "h3";
+
+app.get("/pay", () => {
+  throw problem({
+    status: 403,
+    statusText: "Forbidden",
+    message: "Insufficient balance",
+    type: "https://example.com/probs/balance",
+    instance: "/accounts/123",
+  });
+});
+```
+
+The response uses `content-type: application/problem+json` and this body:
+
+```json
+{
+  "type": "https://example.com/probs/balance",
+  "title": "Forbidden",
+  "status": 403,
+  "detail": "Insufficient balance",
+  "instance": "/accounts/123"
+}
+```
+
+`title` comes from `statusText` and `detail` from `message`; members without a value are omitted. `body` properties are merged at the root as extension members and `data` stays under the `data` key, but neither can override the standard members. A custom `content-type` in `headers` takes precedence. Errors with `unhandled: true` only expose `title` and `status`.
+
+> [!NOTE]
+> Problem details formatting is attached to the error returned by `problem()`. It is lost if the error is copied (for example `new HTTPError({ cause })` or object spread).
+
 ### `HTTPError` Fields
 
 - `status`: HTTP status code in the range 200–599.
